  "worked_solution": "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta )^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +4{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta +4{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8+8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
